00:20:51.720 --> 00:20:52.930
Right.
Is that what you mean?

528
00:20:52.930 --> 00:20:55.550
It gets burned off as, it heats you up actually.

529
00:20:55.550 --> 00:20:56.383
I see.
Yeah.

530
00:20:56.383 --> 00:20:58.040
So it doesn't go towards cooling yourself.

531
00:20:58.040 --> 00:20:59.880
It doesn't do anything productive at all.

532
00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:02.190
It just is more, you're feeding a fire

533
00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:03.440
that is burning too hot.

534
00:21:03.440 --> 00:21:04.480
Right, yeah.

535
00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:07.350
The core temperature thing is such a big deal.

536
00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:12.210
And it's a big reason why so many elite Ironman athletes

537
00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:15.020
have had trouble when it comes to Kona,

538
00:21:15.020 --> 00:21:17.170
because they can dominate in these other races,

539
00:21:17.170 --> 00:21:19.270
and then they get to Kona and the heat and the wind

540
00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:20.730
and all this sort of stuff

541
00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:23.870
and they start running into stomach issues

542
00:21:23.870 --> 00:21:25.048
and overheating issues

543
00:21:25.048 --> 00:21:27.970
that they haven't had to contend with,

544
00:21:27.970 --> 00:21:31.340
and they don't have appropriate fueling strategies for those

545
00:21:31.340 --> 00:21:33.740
because it's so different than these other places.

546
00:21:33.740 --> 00:21:36.470
And so even among the best athletes,

547
00:21:36.470 --> 00:21:39.320
the most elite out there, they've had struggles.

548
00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:40.830
I mean, Chris McCormack went through this,

549
00:21:40.830 --> 00:21:42.310
it took him years to like figure out

550
00:21:42.310 --> 00:21:45.970
how to solve that equation so that he could win that race.

551
00:21:45.970 --> 00:21:48.160
And he's just one of many examples of that.

552
00:21:48.160 --> 00:21:50.520
So I think that's super interesting.

553
00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:55.520
In addition to that, I know that Kristian also wears a CGM,

554
00:21:55.740 --> 00:21:58.580
a constant glucose monitor, is that what it stands for?

555
00:21:58.580 --> 00:22:00.271
Yeah.
Yeah.

556
00:22:00.271 --> 00:22:01.137
Supersapiens.

557
00:22:01.137 --> 00:22:04.680
So they add that like blood glucose detection

558
00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:06.060
into that mix as well.

559
00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:08.810
And then there's this kind of clickbaity thing

560
00:22:08.810 --> 00:22:10.842
about the $2,000 water.

561
00:22:10.842 --> 00:22:12.317
Yes.
What is that about?

562
00:22:12.317 --> 00:22:14.720
So it's a $2,000 water

563
00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:16.840
that's infused with particular isotope

564
00:22:16.840 --> 00:22:18.230
so that when he pees,

565
00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:20.320
they can put it into a mass spectrometer

566
00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:24.393
and find out how well he's metabolizing oxygen.

567
00:22:26.060 --> 00:22:29.010
And then he, they also burn fecal samples

568
00:22:29.010 --> 00:22:33.790
to find out how well he's metabolizing his carbohydrates.

569
00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:37.420
What's funny, it was very cheeky,

570
00:22:37.420 --> 00:22:39.700
like Kristian, sometime last week,

571
00:22:39.700 --> 00:22:41.803
or when did the article came out?

572
00:22:41.803 --> 00:22:43.700
Like 10 days ago or something like that?

573
00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:44.921
Yeah, something like that, two weeks ago, yeah.

574
00:22:44.921 --> 00:22:47.220
When it came out, he shared on Instagram stories

575
00:22:47.220 --> 00:22:49.703
him drinking the $2,000 water,

576
00:22:52.440 --> 00:22:54.470
which I thought was very funny.

577
00:22:54.470 --> 00:22:56.060
But I think it's important to note,

578
00:22:56.060 --> 00:22:57.900
like all this is really interesting,

579
00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:01.370
this is at the very cutting edge of science

580
00:23:01.370 --> 00:23:04.060
and sport performance, and we're talking about

581
00:23:04.060 --> 00:23:07.300
the most elite athletes in the sport.

582
00:23:07.300 --> 00:23:10.570
And I think it's important to contextualize that

583
00:23:10.570 --> 00:23:13.740
against the rest of us who are the huffer/duffers

584
00:23:13.740 --> 00:23:15.290
and the average people.

585
00:23:15.290 --> 00:23:18.620
Lindsay Crouse, former podcast guest, New York Times writer,

586
00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:20.340
did you see the piece that she just wrote?

587
00:23:20.340 --> 00:23:21.380
I did see that, yeah.

588
00:23:21.380 --> 00:23:23.360
About ditching her data.

589
00:23:23.360 --> 00:23:24.380
Like, basically the other,

590
00:23:24.380 --> 00:23:25.710
that's the other end of the spectrum.

591
00:23:25.710 --> 00:23:27.940
Like, I'm done with my watch.

592
00:23:27.940 --> 00:23:31.260
Like, I just wanna go out and enjoy running

593
00:23:31.260 --> 00:23:33.910
in kind of a purest way.

594
00:23:33.910 --> 00:23:36.040
So both of these have their logic.

595
00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:37.690
Like, what is your goal?

596
00:23:37.690 --> 00:23:38.700
Right, yeah, I agree with you.

597
00:23:38.700 --> 00:23:40.930
And just real quick.

598
00:23:40.930 --> 00:23:42.360
If you wanna take a Layman's view,

599
00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:45.476
and if you are interested in getting faster or,

600
00:23:45.476 --> 00:23:48.050
in a scientific way, essentially,

601
00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:50.520
basically what people were doing was

602
00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:53.270
the American side of the sport was looking at VO2 max,

603
00:23:53.270 --> 00:23:55.200
which is kind of your max potential.

604
00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:57.850
And you could hit your max performance,

605
00:23:57.850 --> 00:23:59.950
but maybe only hold it for 30 minutes

606
00:23:59.950 --> 00:24:02.030
or an hour or something like that.

607
00:24:02.030 --> 00:24:05.170
Lactate meters, from what Dan Lorang told me,

608
00:24:05.170 --> 00:24:06.500
was more a European thing.

609
00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:10.400
They were checking out the lactate tolerance in the blood

610
00:24:10.400 --> 00:24:12.680
and that enabled you to go long,

611
00:24:12.680 --> 00:24:15.340
but it wasn't really focused on max performance;

612
00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:17.620
it was more focused on maximum endurance.

613
00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:18.740
And so what Dan did

614
00:24:18.740 --> 00:24:20.720
was mix those two understandings together

615
00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:23.220
to try to expand your max fitness

616
00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:25.440
and hold it for a longer period of time.

617
00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:29.200
And what Olav Aleksander Bu is doing is

618
00:24:29.200 --> 00:24:32.360
is basically taking that and jumping five steps ahead

619
00:24:32.360 --> 00:24:34.720
using more monitors to better understand it.

620
00:24:34.720 --> 00:24:36.077
And Dan says,

621
00:24:36.077 --> 00:24:39.010
"If there's ever a sensor I can't use, he's happy."

622
00:24:39.010 --> 00:24:43.720
So Dan is, and he's coached arguably the great,

623
00:24:43.720 --> 00:24:45.320
certainly the greatest of his generation,

624
00:24:45.320 --> 00:24:47.773
three-time Ironman champion Jan Frodeno,

625
00:24:48.680 --> 00:24:51.220
who's been able to win races deep into his 30s.

626
00:24:51.220 --> 00:24:53.403
I think he's 40 now or something.

627
00:24:54.691 --> 00:24:57.080
So he cares about data.

628
00:24:57.080 --> 00:25:00.620
And Dan and Olav are friends,

629
00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:03.204
and they talk about this stuff a lot.

630
00:25:03.204 --> 00:25:04.037
So I don't know.

631
00:25:04.037 --> 00:25:05.517
I mean, if you're looking at it

632
00:25:05.517 --> 00:25:07.800
from kind of a Layman's perspective,

633
00:25:07.800 --> 00:25:09.040
there's definitely something there

634
00:25:09.040 --> 00:25:12.410
in terms of trying to maximize your best performance rate

635
00:25:12.410 --> 00:25:15.617
and then expand that ability to stay there for a long time.

636
00:25:15.617 --> 00:25:17.350
'Cause we have VO2 max here,

637
00:25:17.350 --> 00:25:18.950
but that doesn't really dictate how fast you go.

638
00:25:18.950 --> 00:25:21.250
I've never paid attention to that ever.

639
00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:23.250
Well, it's not a real one anyway, yes.

640
00:25:23.250 --> 00:25:26.680
But setting my zones based on lactate levels

641
00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:29.720
has always been super important to me.

642
00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:32.520
I talk endlessly about zone two.

643
00:25:32.520 --> 00:25:34.150
But understanding what your zone two is,

644
00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:36.370
which is where you develop your aerobic base

645
00:25:36.370 --> 00:25:38.170
and your aerobic capacity,

646
00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:42.260
you have to be properly lactate-tested

647
00:25:42.260 --> 00:25:44.020
to establish those zones.

648
00:25:44.020 --> 00:25:46.850
And the more aerobically fit you are,

649
00:25:46.850 --> 00:25:48.480
the flatter that curve is

650
00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:51.140
in terms of like the lactate increase

651
00:25:51.140 --> 00:25:56.140
as you escalate your effort and your intensity

652
00:25:56.370 --> 00:25:59.340
and the quicker you kind of go back to baseline.

653
00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:02.200
And in endurance sports, like VO2 max,

654
00:26:02.200 --> 00:26:04.700
I've always thought of it as being not that important

655
00:26:04.700 --> 00:26:08.170
because you're not, what your threshold is

656
00:26:08.170 --> 00:26:09.270
isn't really crucial;

657
00:26:09.270 --> 00:26:13.200
it's how vast your aerobic capacity is.

658
00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:15.100
Like, your ability to maintain

659
00:26:17.713 --> 00:26:20.100
like a subthreshold effort

660
00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:23.650
for the longest period of time possible and recovery quickly

661
00:26:23.650 --> 00:26:26.870
so that when you ramp it up into a tempo effort,

662
00:26:26.870 --> 00:26:30.090
you can then go quickly back down to baseline.

663
00:26:30.090 --> 00:26:32.353
And so what they're trying to do is basically make that--

664
00:26:32.353 --> 00:26:33.660
Work both sides of that.

665
00:26:33.660 --> 00:26:37.790
Yeah, and make that max something they can hold

666
00:26:37.790 --> 00:26:38.720
for a period of time.

667
00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:42.150
But anyway, back to your thing, just to wrap it up.

668
00:26:42.150 --> 00:26:45.890
Kristian won in Tokyo, where the heat was,

669
00:26:45.890 --> 00:26:47.460
obviously it's very well documented

670
00:26:47.460 --> 00:26:50.710
how hot the Olympics were in July in Tokyo.

671
00:26:50.710 --> 00:26:51.960
And he won,

672
00:26:51.960 --> 00:26:54.530
passing runners that were supposed to be faster than him

673
00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:56.960
because he had dialed it back just enough on the bike

674
00:26:56.960 --> 00:26:58.410
and then he just, they were wilting.

675
00:26:58.410 --> 00:27:00.350
And he won the race by 11 seconds.

676
00:27:00.350 --> 00:27:02.950
And the thing that's not in this story that he told me

677
00:27:02.950 --> 00:27:04.170
is he called his shot.

678
00:27:04.170 --> 00:27:06.560
When he was a teenager, when he was 17,

679
00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:08.390
he was interviewed by a local paper in Bergen,

