1556
01:05:19.390 --> 01:05:21.540
So I was like, I'm ignoring this.

1557
01:05:21.540 --> 01:05:22.470
It'll go away.

1558
01:05:22.470 --> 01:05:23.563
This is not real.

1559
01:05:24.400 --> 01:05:28.720
And then the news cycle just continued to move along.

1560
01:05:28.720 --> 01:05:29.850
Like, it got picked up.

1561
01:05:29.850 --> 01:05:32.560
I guess, like, these articles get syndicated

1562
01:05:32.560 --> 01:05:35.190
so then they end up on all these other websites.

1563
01:05:35.190 --> 01:05:36.441
Yeah, the same one ended up on Yahoo.

1564
01:05:36.441 --> 01:05:38.930
They get translated, it was on Yahoo, MSN.

1565
01:05:38.930 --> 01:05:40.320
It just wouldn't go away.

1566
01:05:40.320 --> 01:05:42.179
They're reading about you in Burkina Faso.

1567
01:05:42.179 --> 01:05:45.770
Like on Twitter, there was a local radio DJ

1568
01:05:45.770 --> 01:05:48.060
who did five minutes on it, making fun of me,

1569
01:05:48.060 --> 01:05:50.750
like in some town, I don't even know where, you know.

1570
01:05:50.750 --> 01:05:53.020
I was like, "I can't believe this."

1571
01:05:53.020 --> 01:05:56.350
And then The Young Turks,

1572
01:05:56.350 --> 01:06:00.270
which is a big progressive news YouTube channel,

1573
01:06:00.270 --> 01:06:02.973
they have like five million subscribers,

1574
01:06:04.340 --> 01:06:08.210
well, there's, is it Cenk, Cenk?

1575
01:06:08.210 --> 01:06:10.200
How do you say his name, Cenk Uygur?

1576
01:06:10.200 --> 01:06:12.210
The main guy on The Young Turks.

1577
01:06:12.210 --> 01:06:14.930
But there's these other two pundits,

1578
01:06:14.930 --> 01:06:18.280
Ana Kasparian and John Iadarola,

1579
01:06:18.280 --> 01:06:19.920
who do a show there, I guess.

1580
01:06:19.920 --> 01:06:21.105
I guess.

1581
01:06:21.105 --> 01:06:22.784
And so they did a whole thing--

1582
01:06:22.784 --> 01:06:23.617
I watched that.
Made a video,

1583
01:06:23.617 --> 01:06:25.590
"Why is this husband sleeping alone in a tent,"

1584
01:06:25.590 --> 01:06:27.477
and did like 10 minutes on me

1585
01:06:27.477 --> 01:06:29.983
for this standalone video on The Young Turks.

1586
01:06:29.983 --> 01:06:32.270
There's like over a hundred thousand views

1587
01:06:32.270 --> 01:06:35.912
where they're just making fun of me and riffing about this--

1588
01:06:35.912 --> 01:06:37.169
John is kinda defending you.

1589
01:06:37.169 --> 01:06:38.668
But they didn't even, a little bit.

1590
01:06:38.668 --> 01:06:39.501
He did a little bit.

1591
01:06:39.501 --> 01:06:41.160
She was taking hits at me.

1592
01:06:41.160 --> 01:06:43.089
She was.
But neither of them, like,

1593
01:06:43.089 --> 01:06:44.697
just if you just Googled my name or just like,

1594
01:06:44.697 --> 01:06:47.180
"Oh, he has a podcast," or, "Oh, he wrote a book,"

1595
01:06:47.180 --> 01:06:48.510
or, "Oh, he did these--
Oh, he's bigger than we are.

1596
01:06:48.510 --> 01:06:52.700
No, it was just rich Malibu guy, like, blah, blah, blah.

1597
01:06:52.700 --> 01:06:54.660
And it just, it wasn't like,

1598
01:06:54.660 --> 01:06:57.723
again, I just decided to ignore this whole thing,

1599
01:06:59.580 --> 01:07:01.730
but I think the kinda takeaway--

1600
01:07:01.730 --> 01:07:03.122
It's bizarre, though.
It was weird.

1601
01:07:03.122 --> 01:07:05.620
And it was definitely, like, it could've gone worse.

1602
01:07:05.620 --> 01:07:08.370
And it is this weird feeling of vulnerability.

1603
01:07:08.370 --> 01:07:10.460
I mean, I did make that video, why I sleep in a tent,

1604
01:07:10.460 --> 01:07:12.590
so I put it out there and I told the story on Tim

1605
01:07:12.590 --> 01:07:14.670
and I've talked about it many times here,

1606
01:07:14.670 --> 01:07:16.770
so I'm not hiding anything.

1607
01:07:16.770 --> 01:07:17.603
Yeah, there was also that--

1608
01:07:17.603 --> 01:07:20.300
But there's like when you're at the prey

1609
01:07:20.300 --> 01:07:23.610
of some journalists in their hot take,

1610
01:07:23.610 --> 01:07:26.120
you feel a little bit powerless,

1611
01:07:26.120 --> 01:07:28.580
like, "Oh, they're gonna spin this story

1612
01:07:28.580 --> 01:07:30.150
and I feel defenseless here."

1613
01:07:30.150 --> 01:07:31.260
It is a weird feeling.

1614
01:07:31.260 --> 01:07:33.617
And it made me realize also like,

1615
01:07:33.617 --> 01:07:35.450
"Can't believe what you see or what you read

1616
01:07:35.450 --> 01:07:36.537
or what you," you know.

1617
01:07:36.537 --> 01:07:39.570
It's like, this is just, these people are just doing a job.

1618
01:07:39.570 --> 01:07:41.040
They need to fill this content.

1619
01:07:41.040 --> 01:07:42.040
Like, why are The Young Turks

1620
01:07:42.040 --> 01:07:43.450
even talking about this at all?

1621
01:07:43.450 --> 01:07:47.440
This is not about news or politics or anything like that.

1622
01:07:47.440 --> 01:07:49.300
Was it a slow news day?

1623
01:07:49.300 --> 01:07:51.640
It's just something that's entertaining for people.

1624
01:07:51.640 --> 01:07:56.640
Oh, here's something to, here's some junk fast food story

1625
01:07:57.920 --> 01:08:00.610
for you to chew on for five minutes.

1626
01:08:00.610 --> 01:08:03.180
Well, my biggest takeaway from The Young Turks thing

1627
01:08:03.180 --> 01:08:05.090
is it reminded me why I've never watched

1628
01:08:05.090 --> 01:08:07.440
or listened to The Young Turks.

1629
01:08:07.440 --> 01:08:09.580
Like, I'm not interested.

1630
01:08:09.580 --> 01:08:13.830
But also, there's a difference between journalism,

1631
01:08:13.830 --> 01:08:15.737
actual journalism, and what you've described here.

1632
01:08:15.737 --> 01:08:18.340
And the problem is now people look at that

1633
01:08:18.340 --> 01:08:20.710
and I think that's what they think journalism is.

1634
01:08:20.710 --> 01:08:22.120
And it is, unfortunately.

1635
01:08:22.120 --> 01:08:25.840
Like, you go on CNN and any major news network,

1636
01:08:25.840 --> 01:08:27.270
and it's mostly a bunch of talking heads

1637
01:08:27.270 --> 01:08:28.580
talking about stuff.

1638
01:08:28.580 --> 01:08:31.460
It's not really reporters on the ground doing the hard work.

1639
01:08:31.460 --> 01:08:34.250
And so then we look at The Young Turks

1640
01:08:34.250 --> 01:08:37.330
talking about this story as if that's a story,

1641
01:08:37.330 --> 01:08:39.250
like as if that's a news story

1642
01:08:39.250 --> 01:08:41.290
to be discussed by people and parsed.

1643
01:08:41.290 --> 01:08:46.290
And it's very lazy and bad content

1644
01:08:47.169 --> 01:08:52.169
and strange to go in and take someone's like sleep habits.

1645
01:08:54.410 --> 01:08:55.900
It's cool for you to talk about

1646
01:08:55.900 --> 01:08:57.970
because it's important to you.

1647
01:08:57.970 --> 01:08:59.940
But it's not cool for, like,

1648
01:08:59.940 --> 01:09:01.850
I think it's just bizarre that,

1649
01:09:01.850 --> 01:09:03.890
like, people take these nonstories

1650
01:09:03.890 --> 01:09:08.120
and make them into things just to fill our ears with sound.

1651
01:09:08.120 --> 01:09:08.953
Yeah.

1652
01:09:08.953 --> 01:09:10.923
So anyway, that was, it was,

1653
01:09:11.853 --> 01:09:14.890
I think basically, in the wake of the whole thing,

1654
01:09:14.890 --> 01:09:16.180
I should just, by the way,

1655
01:09:16.180 --> 01:09:18.880
like I've upgraded the tent since then.

1656
01:09:18.880 --> 01:09:20.130
So I got back last night,

1657
01:09:20.130 --> 01:09:22.430
like I got the glamping tent set up.

1658
01:09:22.430 --> 01:09:23.500
It's super dope.

1659
01:09:23.500 --> 01:09:24.333
I've got a bed.

1660
01:09:24.333 --> 01:09:25.450
It's amazing, right?

1661
01:09:25.450 --> 01:09:28.040
So I think I'm just gonna take some photos in the tent

1662
01:09:28.040 --> 01:09:29.669
or in front of the tent--
Enjoy the tent.

1663
01:09:29.669 --> 01:09:30.502
And just say, "Fuck you."

1664
01:09:30.502 --> 01:09:31.710
Yeah, I fucking sleep in the tent.

1665
01:09:31.710 --> 01:09:33.393
Yeah, here's my tent, asshole.

1666
01:09:34.420 --> 01:09:39.420
I also think that, I think of the Willie Nelson phrase,

1667
01:09:41.840 --> 01:09:43.410
where he said, he's told,

1668
01:09:43.410 --> 01:09:44.810
I once interviewed Jack Johnson

1669
01:09:44.810 --> 01:09:46.837
and Willie Nelson told him once,

1670
01:09:46.837 --> 01:09:49.784
"You don't read your press, you weigh it."

1671
01:09:49.784 --> 01:09:52.710
And that, like, in your case, it's like,

1672
01:09:52.710 --> 01:09:54.380
who cares what they're saying about you?

1673
01:09:54.380 --> 01:09:55.347
They're saying something about you

1674
01:09:55.347 --> 01:09:57.200
and that's all that matters.

1675
01:09:57.200 --> 01:09:58.490
Yeah, it's just weird.

1676
01:09:58.490 --> 01:09:59.450
It is weird.
I know.

1677
01:09:59.450 --> 01:10:02.410
Anyway, all right, let's move on.

1678
01:10:02.410 --> 01:10:05.940
I just spent a full month in Maui.

1679
01:10:05.940 --> 01:10:10.200
I flew there on January first, just got back last night.

1680
01:10:10.200 --> 01:10:11.673
Today's the 31st.

1681
01:10:12.640 --> 01:10:17.513
This is the third year that I've taken this sabbatical.

1682
01:10:19.592 --> 01:10:22.120
And so I thought it would be kind of instructive

1683
01:10:22.120 --> 01:10:26.000
to talk a little bit about like why I do this

1684
01:10:26.000 --> 01:10:27.610
and what I've learned.

1685
01:10:27.610 --> 01:10:28.990
And it was just a great experience.

1686
01:10:28.990 --> 01:10:32.770
I mean, it's the most chill and relaxed

1687
01:10:32.770 --> 01:10:34.860
and distant that I've been from work

1688
01:10:34.860 --> 01:10:37.290
in the three years that I've started doing this.

1689
01:10:37.290 --> 01:10:39.890
And I just feel so refreshed and renewed

1690
01:10:39.890 --> 01:10:41.450
from the experience.

1691
01:10:41.450 --> 01:10:43.770
Basically, it was an opportunity

1692
01:10:43.770 --> 01:10:46.950
to completely break ranks with work,

1693
01:10:46.950 --> 01:10:49.373
be off the computer, be off email,

1694
01:10:50.490 --> 01:10:53.280
not have anything to do with anything professional

1695
01:10:53.280 --> 01:10:56.020
and just exercise some self-care.

1696
01:10:56.020 --> 01:10:58.620
So I established like a daily routine.

1697
01:10:58.620 --> 01:11:01.410
I brought my bike, so I would ride every other day.

1698
01:11:01.410 --> 01:11:04.260
And on the days in between, I'd go to the gym,

1699
01:11:04.260 --> 01:11:05.900
and swim, and chill.

1700
01:11:05.900 --> 01:11:09.740
I just feel better than I have in quite a long time,

1701
01:11:09.740 --> 01:11:11.230
physically and mentally.

1702
01:11:11.230 --> 01:11:13.310
And I went to Maui, I was originally,

1703
01:11:13.310 --> 01:11:15.370
I wanted to go to Byron Bay in Australia again,

1704
01:11:15.370 --> 01:11:17.470
but just getting to Australia was too problematic

1705
01:11:17.470 --> 01:11:18.640
with COVID and all of that.

1706
01:11:18.640 --> 01:11:20.700
And Maui was the one Hawaiian island

1707
01:11:20.700 --> 01:11:22.530
that I knew the least about.

1708
01:11:22.530 --> 01:11:24.760
I've spent tons of time on the Big Island,

1709
01:11:24.760 --> 01:11:25.880
tons of time on Kauai.

1710
01:11:25.880 --> 01:11:27.080
I'd been to Maui before.

1711
01:11:27.080 --> 01:11:31.010
I did EPIC5 there and I've been there on one other occasion.

1712
01:11:31.010 --> 01:11:34.150
But I wanted to learn more about that island.

1713
01:11:34.150 --> 01:11:37.739
And now, in retrospect, it's like my favorite island.

1714
01:11:37.739 --> 01:11:38.890
Oh yeah?
It was so great.

1715
01:11:38.890 --> 01:11:39.723
All right.

1716
01:11:39.723 --> 01:11:41.360
First of all, it has the best cycling

1717
01:11:41.360 --> 01:11:44.010
of any island in Hawaii--

1718
01:11:44.010 --> 01:11:46.313
'Cause of the terrain's varied?

1719
01:11:46.313 --> 01:11:48.170
First of all, the pavement is great everywhere,

1720
01:11:48.170 --> 01:11:52.320
which is not the case on some of the other islands.

1721
01:11:52.320 --> 01:11:55.070
But there's a lot of terrain variability,

1722
01:11:55.070 --> 01:11:57.250
tons of coastal rides and climbs,

1723
01:11:57.250 --> 01:12:00.000
and a lot of really interesting routes,

1724
01:12:00.000 --> 01:12:02.200
upcountry, and inland.

1725
01:12:02.200 --> 01:12:05.570
And then, of course, you have the Haleakala Climb,

1726
01:12:05.570 --> 01:12:07.180
which I did last Thursday.

1727
01:12:07.180 --> 01:12:08.690
Did you?

1728
01:12:08.690 --> 01:12:12.080
Yeah, shout out to Jonathan Hostick

1729
01:12:12.080 --> 01:12:14.700
and his beautiful wife, Laura and their kids,

1730
01:12:14.700 --> 01:12:15.760
Marin and Isla.

1731
01:12:15.760 --> 01:12:18.680
So Jonathan was a guy who was out riding one day

1732
01:12:18.680 --> 01:12:23.680
and I was going up this climb and the guy rode down past me,

1733
01:12:24.420 --> 01:12:26.950
but he shouted my name out, I knew he recognized me,

1734
01:12:26.950 --> 01:12:29.270
and he whipped around, we rode a little bit together,

1735
01:12:29.270 --> 01:12:31.260
and he just said, "I'm here for a while.

1736
01:12:31.260 --> 01:12:32.303
I live in Toronto.

1737
01:12:33.340 --> 01:12:34.630
I'm just gonna be riding every day.

1738
01:12:34.630 --> 01:12:37.410
If you wanna ride, and I'm gonna do Haleakala

1739
01:12:37.410 --> 01:12:38.370
probably a couple of times,

1740
01:12:38.370 --> 01:12:43.140
so if you wanna do it, Laura will sag us, crew us."

1741
01:12:43.140 --> 01:12:44.260
Oh, cool.

1742
01:12:44.260 --> 01:12:46.390
So we have some support.

1743
01:12:46.390 --> 01:12:47.480
And I thought that would be cool.

1744
01:12:47.480 --> 01:12:50.040
So last Thursday we did it.

1745
01:12:50.040 --> 01:12:52.710
It's a 34-mile climb,

1746
01:12:52.710 --> 01:12:55.473
where you gain 10,000 feet of elevation.

1747
01:12:55.473 --> 01:12:56.306
Wow.

1748
01:12:56.306 --> 01:12:58.600
This is half assed internet research,

1749
01:12:58.600 --> 01:13:01.010
but I think it's the second longest climb in the world.

1750
01:13:01.010 --> 01:13:02.040
That's what Jonathan said.

1751
01:13:02.040 --> 01:13:04.340
We should probably fact check that.

1752
01:13:04.340 --> 01:13:09.070
But incredible, like, to climb to 10,000 feet.

1753
01:13:09.070 --> 01:13:10.270
And we had this epic day

1754
01:13:10.270 --> 01:13:12.200
where usually it's freezing up there

1755
01:13:13.753 --> 01:13:14.760
when you get to the top,

1756
01:13:14.760 --> 01:13:18.680
but it was like 63 degrees and totally pleasant

1757
01:13:18.680 --> 01:13:20.630
above the cloud line.

1758
01:13:20.630 --> 01:13:22.570
Just extraordinary.

1759
01:13:22.570 --> 01:13:25.670
But that was just one of like many epic rides.

1760
01:13:25.670 --> 01:13:28.260
So I really kind of fell back in love with riding

1761
01:13:28.260 --> 01:13:30.270
'cause I hadn't been cycling that much.

1762
01:13:30.270 --> 01:13:31.370
And because of my back,

1763
01:13:31.370 --> 01:13:33.740
I'm really taking a break from running at the moment,

1764
01:13:33.740 --> 01:13:36.210
but I can ride my bike without it being problematic.

1765
01:13:36.210 --> 01:13:39.840
So it was just fantastic to be on the bike,

1766
01:13:39.840 --> 01:13:41.083
in the warm weather,

1767
01:13:43.284 --> 01:13:48.100
and to really kind of just reset everything.

1768
01:13:48.100 --> 01:13:48.933
And I think--

1769
01:13:48.933 --> 01:13:50.270
Did you swim in the ocean?

1770
01:13:50.270 --> 01:13:51.300
Swim in the ocean.

1771
01:13:51.300 --> 01:13:54.191
There's also a great aquatic center in Kihei.

1772
01:13:54.191 --> 01:13:56.060
I was staying in Kihei.

1773
01:13:56.060 --> 01:13:58.783
Like, multiple eight-lane pools

1774
01:13:58.783 --> 01:14:01.220
and basically not that many people.

1775
01:14:01.220 --> 01:14:02.053
They're free.

1776
01:14:02.053 --> 01:14:03.650
You can just walk in and go.

1777
01:14:03.650 --> 01:14:08.650
So great swimming facility, ocean swimming, cycling--

1778
01:14:08.720 --> 01:14:09.933
Did you cycle to Hana?

1779
01:14:10.850 --> 01:14:11.683
Not all the way.

1780
01:14:11.683 --> 01:14:14.280
I was staying in Kihei,

1781
01:14:14.280 --> 01:14:15.880
which is not where the great cycling is.

1782
01:14:15.880 --> 01:14:19.600
So sometimes I would drive to Paia and then ride,

1783
01:14:19.600 --> 01:14:22.620
I rode like most of the way to Hana one day and rode back,

1784
01:14:22.620 --> 01:14:24.230
but didn't make it all the way there.

1785
01:14:24.230 --> 01:14:29.180
I drove upcountry one day and then road the road to Hana

1786
01:14:29.180 --> 01:14:31.840
on the other side, going the other way,

1787
01:14:31.840 --> 01:14:34.540
which is like you descend for like an hour and a half

1788
01:14:34.540 --> 01:14:37.130
and then you, it's like I better turn around.

1789
01:14:37.130 --> 01:14:41.660
But the grades are like 10 to 13% going back,

1790
01:14:41.660 --> 01:14:43.030
which is pretty steep.

1791
01:14:43.030 --> 01:14:45.380
So all the work is on the way back.

1792
01:14:45.380 --> 01:14:46.920
But I never made it all the way to Hana.

1793
01:14:46.920 --> 01:14:48.040
Okay.

1794
01:14:48.040 --> 01:14:49.135
You need to have like some simple--

1795
01:14:49.135 --> 01:14:50.950
Have you done that?
No, no, no.

1796
01:14:50.950 --> 01:14:53.033
I've been to Hana one time when I was a kid,

1797
01:14:53.033 --> 01:14:54.390
like a long time ago.

1798
01:14:54.390 --> 01:14:59.390
And like, not since I've really been a traveler, so yeah.

1799
01:15:00.690 --> 01:15:02.433
Yeah, I highly recommend it.

1800
01:15:03.550 --> 01:15:06.000
But part of it, of course,

1801
01:15:06.000 --> 01:15:08.540
is to have this analog experience, right,

1802
01:15:08.540 --> 01:15:10.641
and not be on the phone.
Right.

1803
01:15:10.641 --> 01:15:11.474
How'd you do?

1804
01:15:11.474 --> 01:15:15.000
But I didn't go, like, under the idea that I would not,

1805
01:15:15.000 --> 01:15:17.490
I was not bringing my phone with me

1806
01:15:17.490 --> 01:15:19.010
or anything like drastic like that.

1807
01:15:19.010 --> 01:15:22.480
I was like, "I am going to temper my phone use.

1808
01:15:22.480 --> 01:15:24.520
I'm gonna be a responsible iPhone user."

1809
01:15:24.520 --> 01:15:26.913
Did you have like a standard that you were going for,

1810
01:15:26.913 --> 01:15:29.263
like a certain number hours a day or something?

1811
01:15:30.180 --> 01:15:32.230
These guys here were running the business,

1812
01:15:32.230 --> 01:15:33.560
so I wasn't worried about

1813
01:15:33.560 --> 01:15:36.230
having to like be involved in that.

1814
01:15:36.230 --> 01:15:38.790
But I've got a kid who's applying to a school,

1815
01:15:38.790 --> 01:15:40.570
who was gonna need help on the application,

1816
01:15:40.570 --> 01:15:43.610
I'm talking to my family every day, we're FaceTiming,

1817
01:15:43.610 --> 01:15:46.100
there's things that have to get dealt with.

1818
01:15:46.100 --> 01:15:48.860
So the idea was like, "Look, I'll check my email,

1819
01:15:48.860 --> 01:15:50.410
like, maybe once every two days."

1820
01:15:50.410 --> 01:15:52.275
I have an autoresponder up,

1821
01:15:52.275 --> 01:15:56.450
and I'm just gonna limit my phone use as much as possible.

1822
01:15:56.450 --> 01:15:57.920
And I would say that

1823
01:15:59.090 --> 01:16:01.810
in terms of like where reality met aspiration,

1824
01:16:01.810 --> 01:16:05.890
I'd give myself like, I don't know, a B minus--

1825
01:16:05.890 --> 01:16:07.490
Is that because of Wordle?
Maybe a C plus.

1826
01:16:07.490 --> 01:16:08.673
Would you have gotten an A without Wordle?

1827
01:16:08.673 --> 01:16:10.503
Wordle was a problem.

1828
01:16:10.503 --> 01:16:11.400
(Adam laughs)

1829
01:16:11.400 --> 01:16:12.870
Wordle was indulgent, though.

1830
01:16:12.870 --> 01:16:14.610
Wordle's self-care, buddy.

1831
01:16:14.610 --> 01:16:16.580
Yeah, I'm not worried about Wordle.

1832
01:16:16.580 --> 01:16:18.504
It's all the other stuff.

1833
01:16:18.504 --> 01:16:20.160
Just (mumbles) Wordle on your Twitter, that's all.

1834
01:16:20.160 --> 01:16:23.097
And there were times where I found myself like,

1835
01:16:23.097 --> 01:16:25.850
"Why am I sitting here scrolling Twitter,"

1836
01:16:25.850 --> 01:16:29.780
like, "This is my opportunity to not do that."

1837
01:16:29.780 --> 01:16:32.530
And I have no excuse and no reason.

1838
01:16:32.530 --> 01:16:36.470
But that allure, that pull is so strong.

1839
01:16:36.470 --> 01:16:38.760
But then it becomes like this spiral of,

1840
01:16:38.760 --> 01:16:40.170
then you criticize yourself for doing it

1841
01:16:40.170 --> 01:16:42.477
and then you, like, beat yourself up.

1842
01:16:42.477 --> 01:16:43.880
(Adam laughs)

1843
01:16:43.880 --> 01:16:45.227
I'm an expert at that.

1844
01:16:45.227 --> 01:16:46.645
I'm an expert at that.

1845
01:16:46.645 --> 01:16:47.478
Wait, what am I doing?

1846
01:16:47.478 --> 01:16:48.510
I'm wasting my time.

1847
01:16:48.510 --> 01:16:49.790
It's hard, man.

1848
01:16:49.790 --> 01:16:50.838
It's hard.

1849
01:16:50.838 --> 01:16:52.470
And then you come up with these justifications

1850
01:16:52.470 --> 01:16:54.260
or these rationalizations

1851
01:16:54.260 --> 01:16:58.994
and then you're just in this like mental looping spiral

1852
01:16:58.994 --> 01:17:00.530
of like negative emotions.

1853
01:17:00.530 --> 01:17:02.800
And then you're like, "What am I doing?

1854
01:17:02.800 --> 01:17:06.137
I'm supposed to be off of this fucking thing."

1855
01:17:08.810 --> 01:17:10.930
Which is interesting because I have this book here

1856
01:17:10.930 --> 01:17:14.230
sitting on my counter "Stolen Focus" by Johann Hari,

1857
01:17:14.230 --> 01:17:17.457
who's coming in I think next week to talk about it.

1858
01:17:17.457 --> 01:17:19.370
So I'm about halfway through this book.

1859
01:17:19.370 --> 01:17:22.690
And this book is about this exact thing.

1860
01:17:22.690 --> 01:17:25.690
And part of it is it's not our fault.

1861
01:17:25.690 --> 01:17:28.260
Like, these things are so powerful

1862
01:17:28.260 --> 01:17:33.260
that we are powerless to basically rebut their wiles.

1863
01:17:34.090 --> 01:17:35.750
And it is really difficult.

1864
01:17:35.750 --> 01:17:37.237
And this book is great.

1865
01:17:37.237 --> 01:17:40.950
It's kind of about Johann's own version of this experience

1866
01:17:40.950 --> 01:17:45.950
and also the science behind how our focus is being eroded

1867
01:17:47.990 --> 01:17:50.950
as a result of technology and kind of our modern world.

1868
01:17:50.950 --> 01:17:52.990
And I look forward to talking to him.

1869
01:17:52.990 --> 01:17:55.200
I always love talking to him about it

1870
01:17:55.200 --> 01:17:56.240
when he comes onto the show,

1871
01:17:56.240 --> 01:17:59.470
but I was having my own like lived experience

1872
01:17:59.470 --> 01:18:02.170
with this problem that I think is relatable to everybody

1873
01:18:02.170 --> 01:18:04.700
who's ever tried to put the phone away.

1874
01:18:04.700 --> 01:18:07.730
But when you strip away all of the excuses,

1875
01:18:07.730 --> 01:18:10.400
like I have no work excuse,

1876
01:18:10.400 --> 01:18:13.550
I am here for the specific purpose

1877
01:18:13.550 --> 01:18:18.550
of kind of simplifying my life and getting clarity,

1878
01:18:19.250 --> 01:18:21.660
and to have it creep into that,

1879
01:18:21.660 --> 01:18:24.910
like, makes me realize just how pernicious it is

1880
01:18:24.910 --> 01:18:27.620
and how difficult it is to create healthy boundaries

1881
01:18:27.620 --> 01:18:28.780
around the phone.

1882
01:18:28.780 --> 01:18:30.690
So for the most part, I did fine,

1883
01:18:30.690 --> 01:18:33.510
but I had these like lapses that made me realize

1884
01:18:33.510 --> 01:18:35.610
like how powerless I can be at times

1885
01:18:35.610 --> 01:18:38.233
when it comes to this kind of stuff.

1886
01:18:39.980 --> 01:18:41.070
But in terms of what I learned,

1887
01:18:41.070 --> 01:18:43.830
like that's sort of the negative aspect of this.

1888
01:18:43.830 --> 01:18:45.300
I mean, for me,

1889
01:18:45.300 --> 01:18:49.610
like, it was great to not be on social media.

1890
01:18:49.610 --> 01:18:52.790
Like, I didn't post anything about being there.

1891
01:18:52.790 --> 01:18:55.280
I didn't really want anyone to know that I was there.

1892
01:18:55.280 --> 01:18:58.180
I really didn't wanna have any complexities.

1893
01:18:58.180 --> 01:18:59.610
I am a true introvert.

1894
01:18:59.610 --> 01:19:02.630
And I realized that I had no problem ostensibly

1895
01:19:02.630 --> 01:19:05.950
being entirely alone for this entire month

1896
01:19:05.950 --> 01:19:07.810
and barely talking to anybody.

1897
01:19:07.810 --> 01:19:10.100
Like, I was totally fine with that.

1898
01:19:10.100 --> 01:19:12.710
I needed that to kind of like heal

1899
01:19:12.710 --> 01:19:15.650
and restore my energy levels.

1900
01:19:15.650 --> 01:19:18.350
I also realized, and I've learned this in the past,

1901
01:19:18.350 --> 01:19:23.350
but this idea that, the way that our culture is set up,

1902
01:19:24.390 --> 01:19:27.980
you go on vacation for a week or maybe two weeks,

1903
01:19:27.980 --> 01:19:31.690
but I didn't even feel myself slowing down

1904
01:19:31.690 --> 01:19:33.350
until I was like in the third week.

1905
01:19:33.350 --> 01:19:34.530
Yes, it takes two weeks.

1906
01:19:34.530 --> 01:19:35.890
It takes two weeks.
It takes longer.

1907
01:19:35.890 --> 01:19:38.360
And I realized how privileged I am

1908
01:19:38.360 --> 01:19:43.360
and how special this opportunity was

1909
01:19:43.400 --> 01:19:45.620
that I was able to take advantage of.

1910
01:19:45.620 --> 01:19:48.790
But the idea that you're gonna go away for a couple days

1911
01:19:48.790 --> 01:19:52.460
and come back refreshed is ludicrous.

1912
01:19:52.460 --> 01:19:54.560
It takes two weeks to get into a new state of being.

1913
01:19:54.560 --> 01:19:55.980
And then if you come home right after it,

1914
01:19:55.980 --> 01:19:57.790
you never really explore that state of being.

1915
01:19:57.790 --> 01:19:59.840
So then you start to unwind in the third week.

1916
01:19:59.840 --> 01:20:02.290
The unwinding doesn't start until like day 15, 16.

1917
01:20:02.290 --> 01:20:04.240
Sure, yeah, yeah.

1918
01:20:04.240 --> 01:20:07.420
And it's not then until, it's not until then

1919
01:20:07.420 --> 01:20:09.790
that you even begin to get any clarity at all

1920
01:20:09.790 --> 01:20:11.352
on like how you're living your life.

1921
01:20:11.352 --> 01:20:12.453
100%.

1922
01:20:12.453 --> 01:20:15.180
So I have another thought on that that I wanna share.

1923
01:20:15.180 --> 01:20:16.693
Let me put a pin in that.

1924
01:20:17.690 --> 01:20:20.960
But I also, I learned that I like routine,

1925
01:20:20.960 --> 01:20:22.020
even when I don't need it.

1926
01:20:22.020 --> 01:20:23.050
Like, even when you're in a place

1927
01:20:23.050 --> 01:20:24.950
where it's like you don't need routine.

1928
01:20:24.950 --> 01:20:26.137
I was happier when I was like,

1929
01:20:26.137 --> 01:20:30.150
"Okay, my routine is I'm gonna ride my bike every other day

1930
01:20:30.150 --> 01:20:32.040
and I'm gonna go to gym gym on the other days."

1931
01:20:32.040 --> 01:20:36.180
But also, to say, "I'm not here on a training camp.

1932
01:20:36.180 --> 01:20:38.320
I don't have to be agenda-driven,"

1933
01:20:38.320 --> 01:20:40.920
but to have some kind of structure,

1934
01:20:40.920 --> 01:20:44.570
I don't know, for some reason made me feel better

1935
01:20:44.570 --> 01:20:46.827
about the experience than just being like,

1936
01:20:46.827 --> 01:20:51.827
"I'm just gonna like not have any schedule whatsoever."

1937
01:20:51.980 --> 01:20:54.450
So it's that balance of like having some kind of routine

1938
01:20:54.450 --> 01:20:58.253
while also not overscheduling things.

1939
01:21:00.390 --> 01:21:05.390
I learned that the world spins in my absence,

1940
01:21:07.100 --> 01:21:09.210
which is great for the control issues

1941
01:21:09.210 --> 01:21:12.690
and a healthy dose of humility that I'm not that important.

1942
01:21:12.690 --> 01:21:13.523
That was good.

1943
01:21:15.230 --> 01:21:18.400
I mentioned that, yes, I fell back in love with cycling.

1944
01:21:18.400 --> 01:21:20.990
I don't think that I could ever live on an island.

1945
01:21:20.990 --> 01:21:22.010
No?
By the time

1946
01:21:22.010 --> 01:21:24.330
the month came up, I was ready to come back.

1947
01:21:24.330 --> 01:21:25.163
What about the island?

1948
01:21:25.163 --> 01:21:27.820
So despite all the introverted tendencies that I have,

1949
01:21:27.820 --> 01:21:32.610
like, I really missed people and it was time to reconnect.

1950
01:21:32.610 --> 01:21:35.940
But I think to go back to that point that I put a pin in,

1951
01:21:35.940 --> 01:21:39.810
the biggest thing that, I think, I took away

1952
01:21:39.810 --> 01:21:42.750
that I've learned from this experience

1953
01:21:42.750 --> 01:21:46.750
is when you get to that, like when I'm in that third week

1954
01:21:46.750 --> 01:21:50.057
and I have put enough distance between me

1955
01:21:50.057 --> 01:21:52.640
and these kind of work patterns

1956
01:21:52.640 --> 01:21:54.680
to have some level of clarity,

1957
01:21:54.680 --> 01:21:59.170
I found myself confronting this default setting

1958
01:21:59.170 --> 01:22:03.610
that I have to always be like achieving, producing,

1959
01:22:03.610 --> 01:22:05.588
progressing, like even,

1960
01:22:05.588 --> 01:22:07.330
like, "Okay, I'm gonna ride my bike.

1961
01:22:07.330 --> 01:22:09.820
Well, I should ride harder or longer than I did last time,"

1962
01:22:09.820 --> 01:22:13.260
or like, "What am I trying to, what is my angle here,

1963
01:22:13.260 --> 01:22:14.810
how am I gonna make myself better

1964
01:22:14.810 --> 01:22:16.040
through the result of this?"

1965
01:22:16.040 --> 01:22:17.670
Like, wait a minute,

1966
01:22:17.670 --> 01:22:19.370
that's not what this experience is about.

1967
01:22:19.370 --> 01:22:21.350
You're not at a training camp.

1968
01:22:21.350 --> 01:22:23.250
You don't have to be agenda-driven.

1969
01:22:23.250 --> 01:22:24.603
Can you just be?

1970
01:22:26.170 --> 01:22:29.430
And that's the hardest part of all, right?

1971
01:22:29.430 --> 01:22:32.500
Like, can you just sit with yourself

1972
01:22:32.500 --> 01:22:36.970
and be in the allowing of experience

1973
01:22:36.970 --> 01:22:39.250
without it being connected to

1974
01:22:39.250 --> 01:22:44.250
or tied to some trajectory that's moving you

1975
01:22:46.890 --> 01:22:49.553
in a certain direction or in accordance--

1976
01:22:49.553 --> 01:22:50.559
No, no, I can't.

1977
01:22:50.559 --> 01:22:53.160
Yeah, it's like, and it was amazing to me,

1978
01:22:53.160 --> 01:22:54.683
like I would notice that,

1979
01:22:57.210 --> 01:23:00.180
and then I would really begin to appreciate

1980
01:23:00.180 --> 01:23:02.610
like how strong that impulse it was.

1981
01:23:02.610 --> 01:23:03.710
It's like, "Oh my God."

1982
01:23:03.710 --> 01:23:05.460
Every time I'm like, "Oh, I should,"

1983
01:23:05.460 --> 01:23:06.420
or even when I was like, "You know what,

1984
01:23:06.420 --> 01:23:07.620
I'm a little tired today,

1985
01:23:07.620 --> 01:23:10.170
like maybe I'll just hang out here and read.

1986
01:23:10.170 --> 01:23:11.630
No, you should drive to Hana.

1987
01:23:11.630 --> 01:23:13.810
No, you should, there's that one trail

1988
01:23:13.810 --> 01:23:14.780
you haven't checked out yet.

1989
01:23:14.780 --> 01:23:15.810
You better go check it out,

1990
01:23:15.810 --> 01:23:17.003
because I don't know if you're gonna have time to do it."

1991
01:23:17.003 --> 01:23:18.950
It's like all of that, right?

1992
01:23:18.950 --> 01:23:22.480
Like, it's all tied to how am I progressing,

1993
01:23:22.480 --> 01:23:25.040
how am I improving myself, what am I missing,

1994
01:23:25.040 --> 01:23:28.780
what is the thing that I'm gonna like beat myself up for

1995
01:23:28.780 --> 01:23:29.880
if I don't do it?

1996
01:23:29.880 --> 01:23:33.440
And realizing how ingrained that is, I think,

1997
01:23:33.440 --> 01:23:36.053
was the biggest kind of epiphany in the whole thing.

1998
01:23:37.350 --> 01:23:39.230
Yeah, I mean, I think that's pretty,

1999
01:23:39.230 --> 01:23:41.303
that kind of epiphany isn't,

2000
01:23:42.150 --> 01:23:44.630
yeah, I mean, that's the unwinding, right?

2001
01:23:44.630 --> 01:23:45.880
That's the whole idea.

2002
01:23:45.880 --> 01:23:47.230
That's why you did it, right?

2003
01:23:47.230 --> 01:23:49.517
That, I think, is why you did it, right?

2004
01:23:49.517 --> 01:23:51.030
And that's why you keep doing it

2005
01:23:51.030 --> 01:23:54.120
because that unwinding is so critical.

2006
01:23:54.120 --> 01:23:58.460
I remember, April and I did a delayed kinda extra honeymoon,

2007
01:23:58.460 --> 01:24:02.470
'cause we did this very quick ceremony and then we did,

2008
01:24:02.470 --> 01:24:03.510
we didn't celebrate it for a while

2009
01:24:03.510 --> 01:24:05.430
'cause of work commitments,

2010
01:24:05.430 --> 01:24:10.430
and then we went on a trip to Fiji, Tonga, and Australia.

2011
01:24:13.220 --> 01:24:15.437
And it was mostly a dive trip

2012
01:24:15.437 --> 01:24:17.180
and to reconnect with her homeland.

2013
01:24:17.180 --> 01:24:20.500
And then when we were first planning it,

2014
01:24:20.500 --> 01:24:23.520
like, she was planning it for like 10 weeks

2015
01:24:23.520 --> 01:24:24.353
or something like that

2016
01:24:24.353 --> 01:24:26.900
and I scaled it back to like six weeks.

2017
01:24:26.900 --> 01:24:29.507
And then about two weeks in, I turned to her and I'm like,

2018
01:24:29.507 --> 01:24:31.364
"Why did I tell us to come home?"

2019
01:24:31.364 --> 01:24:34.820
Like, once you're out, it's good to stay out.

2020
01:24:34.820 --> 01:24:36.793
Like, once you're unwound,

2021
01:24:37.860 --> 01:24:39.850
it doesn't matter how long you're gone.

2022
01:24:39.850 --> 01:24:40.683
'Cause like you said,

2023
01:24:40.683 --> 01:24:42.300
the world's always here when you get back.

2024
01:24:42.300 --> 01:24:44.380
I don't regret coming back early.

2025
01:24:44.380 --> 01:24:45.850
But like knowing that,

2026
01:24:45.850 --> 01:24:47.870
it's something I'll never forget next time

2027
01:24:47.870 --> 01:24:49.910
'cause it's like you can always be,

2028
01:24:49.910 --> 01:24:51.440
it could always be a little longer.

2029
01:24:51.440 --> 01:24:52.273
That's for sure.

2030
01:24:52.273 --> 01:24:55.823
Although I would say that I was ready to come back.

2031
01:24:55.823 --> 01:24:57.750
I was excited to get back.

2032
01:24:57.750 --> 01:24:59.550
I missed me some Skolnick.

2033
01:24:59.550 --> 01:25:00.383
Dude.

2034
01:25:00.383 --> 01:25:01.216
I miss the guys here.

2035
01:25:01.216 --> 01:25:03.050
I miss my family.
We need you here.

2036
01:25:03.050 --> 01:25:06.290
And now I'm enthusiastic to be back.

2037
01:25:06.290 --> 01:25:08.374
These guys were a mess without you.

2038
01:25:08.374 --> 01:25:09.700
I mean, they did their jobs.
These guys are just fine.

2039
01:25:09.700 --> 01:25:12.210
But there was a lot of sobbing.

2040
01:25:12.210 --> 01:25:14.146
Although Blake's walking around with a cane,

2041
01:25:14.146 --> 01:25:14.979
something about his back.

2042
01:25:14.979 --> 01:25:16.580
It is a cane or a trekking pole?

2043
01:25:16.580 --> 01:25:19.034
It's a trekking pole masquerading as a cane.

2044
01:25:19.034 --> 01:25:20.834
(both laugh)

2045
01:25:20.834 --> 01:25:22.540
It's an emotionally--

2046
01:25:22.540 --> 01:25:26.233
Something about a bad back from all those ultras.

2047
01:25:27.270 --> 01:25:28.103
I don't know.

2048
01:25:28.103 --> 01:25:29.550
He's chuckling back there.

2049
01:25:29.550 --> 01:25:30.490
All right, let's move on.

2050
01:25:30.490 --> 01:25:32.870
We're gonna share a couple pieces of content, right?

2051
01:25:32.870 --> 01:25:33.703
Yeah, yeah, we are.

2052
01:25:33.703 --> 01:25:35.310
Enjoying?
Yeah.

2053
01:25:35.310 --> 01:25:37.120
You wanna go first?

2054
01:25:37.120 --> 01:25:40.280
Well, I think, well, I was,

2055
01:25:40.280 --> 01:25:42.280
I think my content, I have here,

2056
01:25:42.280 --> 01:25:45.368
is keying off of the Alan Watts stuff

2057
01:25:45.368 --> 01:25:46.610
that you wanted to post.
Yeah, so I'll start.

2058
01:25:46.610 --> 01:25:51.610
I mean, the first thing that I wanted to draw attention to

2059
01:25:51.660 --> 01:25:56.350
is this catalog of talks by Alan Watts

2060
01:25:56.350 --> 01:26:01.183
that Sam Harris has made available on his Waking Up app.

2061
01:26:02.447 --> 01:26:04.110
And I spent a lot of time with Alan Watts

2062
01:26:04.110 --> 01:26:06.550
over the course of my sabbatical.

2063
